1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,187
Bear's Notes
Why a Dim Blue Lamp Beats a Blinding Red One

2
00:00:03,370 --> 00:00:07,823
Aim the brightest red lamp you own at a metal plate — and not one electron escapes.

3
00:00:08,150 --> 00:00:12,337
Swap in a dim blue glow, and electrons fly off the instant it lands.

4
00:00:12,560 --> 00:00:17,213
Crank the red lamp up to blinding, pour on more and more light — and still, nothing.

5
00:00:17,520 --> 00:00:21,773
Brightness is energy, so more ought to mean more — yet here it means nothing at all.

6
00:00:21,960 --> 00:00:28,713
A wave washes its energy smoothly over every electron, so enough brightness should eventually shake any one of them loose.

7
00:00:29,070 --> 00:00:36,790
But below a certain color, no brightness and no patience ever free a single electron — so the smooth-wave picture is simply wrong here.

8
00:00:37,010 --> 00:00:41,263
So stop picturing a smooth wave, and picture light arriving as separate packets.

9
00:00:41,560 --> 00:00:47,613
Each packet carries a fixed energy set only by its color — red packets weak, blue packets strong.

10
00:00:47,910 --> 00:00:51,797
Turn up the brightness and you get more packets per second, never fatter ones.

11
00:00:52,090 --> 00:00:57,877
Every electron sits in the metal behind a fixed escape cost it must be handed all at once to break free.

12
00:00:58,120 --> 00:01:02,440
And a packet is swallowed whole by one electron — never shared as sips among many.

13
00:01:02,670 --> 00:01:08,557
A weak red packet lands, falls short of the escape cost, and its energy just bleeds away as heat.

14
00:01:08,780 --> 00:01:14,333
Pour on millions of red packets and every one still falls short — a flood of near-misses frees nothing.

15
00:01:14,530 --> 00:01:21,017
Now one faint blue packet lands, clears the escape cost in a single hit, and an electron flies free.

16
00:01:21,300 --> 00:01:25,520
Whatever energy is left after paying the cost becomes that electron's speed.

17
00:01:25,850 --> 00:01:35,337
So a packet's energy is just a constant times its frequency — write it E equals h nu — and that packet of light has a name: a photon.

18
00:01:35,720 --> 00:01:44,240
An electron escapes only when h nu beats the cost, and the leftover is its kinetic energy: K equals h nu minus the escape cost.

19
00:01:44,580 --> 00:01:51,333
Below the frequency where h nu exactly equals the cost, no packet can ever pay — that's the threshold color.

20
00:01:51,500 --> 00:01:58,287
Now the mystery dissolves: brightness only counts the packets, but color decides whether any single packet can pay.

21
00:01:58,500 --> 00:02:05,287
A blinding red lamp is a torrent of packets that each fall short; a dim blue lamp is a trickle that each clear the bar.

22
00:02:05,500 --> 00:02:14,787
On sodium, ultraviolet at three hundred nanometers brings four point one electron volts against a two point three cost, and electrons leave with energy to spare.

23
00:02:15,010 --> 00:02:23,163
Slide to green, and each packet lands a hair below the cost — now not one electron leaves, not even under a ten thousand watt lamp.

24
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:30,353
We didn't measure the constant h itself, or ask how one packet can also behave like a wave — each is its own video.

25
00:02:30,640 --> 00:02:36,893
Try it yourself: look up a metal's escape cost and find the longest wavelength of light that can still free an electron.

26
00:02:37,280 --> 00:02:43,433
Thanks for watching Why a Dim Blue Lamp Beats a Blinding Red One — more at youtube dot com, slash, at Nik Bear Brown.
